Hi,

Thank for your answer... however, I need to further with a slightly more complex example where the server.R includes code that is based upon the different slots of the conductor. The following returns:

Error in .getReactiveEnvironment()$currentContext() :
  Operation not allowed without an active reactive context. (You tried to do something that can only be done from inside a reactive expression or observer.)

I guess that my if statement needs somehow to be transformed...

A more general question is: what are the rules to use conductors in a similar way as one would use regular R objects (list, matrix, dataframe, or other classes)?

library(shiny)
library(shinydashboard)

myf <- function(x){
  list(a=x,
       b=letters[1:10],
       c=TRUE)
}

server <- function(input, output, session) {
  myconductor <- reactive(myf(1))

  if (myconductor()$c){
    output$component <- renderText(paste(myconductor()$a, collapse=','))
  } else {
    output$component <- renderText(paste(myconductor()$b, collapse=','))

  }
}

ui <- dashboardPage(
  header=dashboardHeader(),
  sidebar=dashboardSidebar(),
  body=dashboardBody(
    verbatimTextOutput('component')
  ),
  title='Test',
  skin='blue'
)

shinyApp(ui = ui, server = server)

