Hi,

Please consider the following example code (modified from the article from the Shiny website). Upon clicking on the point in the plot, the info reactive conductor should contain a data.frame with one row but nothing happens in the app. The output functions contained in the renderUI calls look like they do not see any data in the info() reactive.

I am assuming that the renderUI are run as the error message shown prior to the click disappears upon clicking. The fact that I do not see my dummy data.frame in myUI makes me believe that info() is not null.and contain at least one row of data.. so why does it shown by the output functions?

Any suggestion will be welcome.

Thank you

PS: I know that the initial code in the Shiny website article works by directly rendering the result of the nearPoint call, but I need to create a reactive to make my UI conditional... hence, my question.

library(shiny)
library(ggplot2)

ui <- basicPage(
  plotOutput("plot1", click = "plot_click"),
  uiOutput("myUI"),
  uiOutput("debug")
)

server <- function(input, output) {
  output$plot1 <- renderPlot({
    ggplot(mtcars, aes(x=wt, y=mpg)) + geom_point()
  })

  info <- reactive({
    sel <- nearPoints(mtcars, input$plot_click, threshold = 10, maxpoints = 1,
               addDist = TRUE)
    if (nrow(sel)==0 | is.null(sel))
      return(NULL)
    sel
  })

  output$myUI <- renderUI({
    if (is.null(info()) | nrow(info())==0)
      return(data.frame(dummy=TRUE))
    tableOutput(info())
  })

  output$debug <- renderUI({
    verbatimTextOutput(str(info()))
  })

}

shinyApp(ui, server)

